\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 0. ABSOLUTE LOCATION AND SCENE-IDENTITY RULE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 LOCATION ACCURACY HAS HIGHER PRIORITY THAN DESIGN COMPLETENESS.
\f3\b0 \
However:\

\f0\b THE FINAL ARTWORK SHOULD STILL CONTAIN A MEANINGFUL SCENE IDENTITY.
\f3\b0 \
The model must therefore distinguish between:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 A \'97 USER-PROVIDED LOCATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 The user explicitly provides a:\
country,\uc0\u8232 province,\u8232 city,\u8232 district,\u8232 landmark,\u8232 park,\u8232 lake,\u8232 station,\u8232 building,\u8232 street,\u8232 temple,\u8232 garden,\u8232 scenic area,\u8232 or other place name.\
This information has the 
\f0\b highest priority
\f3\b0 .\
Unless the photograph obviously contradicts it, use the user's supplied place identity exactly.\
If the user says:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Ningbo Moon Lake
\f3\b0 \
do not replace it with:\
Hangzhou West Lake,\uc0\u8232 Suzhou,\u8232 another Chinese lake,\u8232 or another visually similar famous destination.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 B \'97 VERIFIED LOCATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 The user did not provide a place name, but the photograph contains sufficiently reliable evidence.\
Examples:\
clearly readable signs,\uc0\u8232 station names,\u8232 building names,\u8232 park plaques,\u8232 road signs,\u8232 official inscriptions,\u8232 unique landmark structures,\u8232 or multiple independent structural clues.\
Only the geographic level actually supported by evidence may be used.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 C \'97 PLAUSIBLE LOCATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 The photograph visually resembles a known place, but the evidence is insufficient.\
Examples:\
\'93looks like West Lake\'94\
\'93resembles Kyoto\'94\
\'93probably Suzhou\'94\
\'93possibly Shanghai\'94\
\'93feels like a Japanese garden\'94\
These are 
\f0\b NOT verified geographic identities
\f3\b0 .\
Do not present them as facts.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 D \'97 UNVERIFIED LOCATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 No reliable geographic location can be established.\
In this case:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 DO NOT INVENT A PLACE NAME.
\f3\b0 \
Instead, identify and write the 
\f0\b observable scene itself
\f3\b0 .\
For example:\
WATERSIDE GARDEN SCENE\
URBAN RIVERSIDE WALK\
LAKESIDE BOAT SCENE\
TREE-LINED WATERFRONT\
HISTORIC-STYLE GARDEN SCENE\
MOUNTAIN ROAD STUDY\
COASTAL STATION SCENE\
TEMPLE COURTYARD STUDY\
SUMMER STREET OBSERVATION\
RIVERSIDE ARCHITECTURAL STUDY\
The scene title must describe 
\f0\b what is visibly present
\f3\b0 , not where the model imagines it might be.\
Therefore:\

\f0\b UNKNOWN LOCATION DOES NOT MEAN EMPTY TYPOGRAPHY.
\f3\b0 \
It means:\

\f0\b USE A TRUTHFUL SCENE IDENTITY INSTEAD OF A FABRICATED GEOGRAPHIC IDENTITY.
\f3\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
